language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ jobshop: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Several jobs are processed on several machines. Each job is a sequence of tasks that must be performed in its given order, and each task must be processed on a specific machine. The tasks are to be scheduled so as to minimise the length of the schedule, the time at which all the jobs are completed (the makespan). A machine works on only one task at a time, and a task, once started, runs to completion.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `jobshop.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/jobshop.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `jobshop_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py jobshop.json
$   savilerow model.eprime jobshop_1.param -run-solver

$ number of jobs, derived from the data: the number of rows of jobs_data (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given jobs_data_dim1 : int
$ largest number of tasks of any job, derived from the data (the padded row length; the reader's JSON has no such field)
given jobs_data_dim2 : int
$ number of tasks of each job, derived from the data (the reader's JSON has no such field)
given jobs_data_len : matrix indexed by [int(1..jobs_data_dim1)] of int
$ jobs_data[j,k,1]: machine of task k of job j (machines 0-based, as in the data); jobs_data[j,k,2]: duration of that task; indices 1-based. Entries beyond jobs_data_len[j] are padding and hold 0.
given jobs_data : matrix indexed by [int(1..jobs_data_dim1), int(1..jobs_data_dim2), int(1..2)] of int(0..)

$ an upper bound on the makespan: the total duration of all the tasks (padding adds nothing)
letting horizon = sum j : int(1..jobs_data_dim1) . sum k : int(1..jobs_data_dim2) . jobs_data[j,k,2]

$ start[j,k] (auxiliary; only the makespan is asked for): the time at which task k of job j starts
find start : matrix indexed by [int(1..jobs_data_dim1), int(1..jobs_data_dim2)] of int(0..horizon)
$ makespan: the time at which all the jobs are completed
find makespan : int(0..horizon)

branching on [start]

minimising makespan
such that
$ each task of a job starts only after the previous task of that job is completed
forAll j : int(1..jobs_data_dim1) . forAll k : int(1..jobs_data_dim2 - 1) . ((k + 1 <= jobs_data_len[j]) -> (start[j,k] + jobs_data[j,k,2] <= start[j,k+1])),
$ the makespan is at least the completion time of every task
forAll j : int(1..jobs_data_dim1) . forAll k : int(1..jobs_data_dim2) . ((k <= jobs_data_len[j]) -> (start[j,k] + jobs_data[j,k,2] <= makespan)),
$ a machine works on only one task at a time: two distinct tasks on the same machine do not overlap in time
forAll j1 : int(1..jobs_data_dim1) . forAll k1 : int(1..jobs_data_dim2) . forAll j2 : int(1..jobs_data_dim1) . forAll k2 : int(1..jobs_data_dim2) . (((j1 < j2) \/ ((j1 = j2) /\ (k1 < k2))) /\ (k1 <= jobs_data_len[j1]) /\ (k2 <= jobs_data_len[j2]) /\ (jobs_data[j1,k1,1] = jobs_data[j2,k2,1])) -> ((start[j1,k1] + jobs_data[j1,k1,2] <= start[j2,k2]) \/ (start[j2,k2] + jobs_data[j2,k2,2] <= start[j1,k1]))
